  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on retrospective survey data from a single city, Chicago, which may introduce recall bias and limit generalizability to other urban contexts.",
      "what": "This study analyzed how neighborhood crime, built environment, and life events influence household vehicle purchases, body types, and vintage choices using a 10-year retrospective survey from Chicago. A nested logit model was used to link household decisions to violent and property crime data, socio-demographics, and transit access. The analysis found that exposure to crime increases the likelihood of purchasing a vehicle and shifts preferences specifically toward used vehicles, while higher residential density and transit access decrease purchase probability.",
      "why": "The study establishes a direct empirical link between neighborhood crime exposure and vehicle ownership decisions, demonstrating that personal safety concerns drive car dependency even when transit and density are present."
